# Inspect [[.Name]]

Read `project.json` and the actual API contract. The starter `/health` binding is
an example; replace it with a real JSON endpoint. The installed Loop runs this
manifest directly, with no Go module or standalone build:

```sh
loop check --manifest project.json
loop run --manifest project.json -- describe
loop run --manifest project.json -- status get
```

Bindings support GET, string inputs and complete JSON results. `http.query` maps
input names to API query names. Supply input with `--input '{"name":"value"}'`.
Credentials come from `[[.BaseURLEnv]]` and `[[.TokenEnv]]`; never copy their values
into source or diagnostics. Base URL path prefixes are preserved.

Reuse the host project's tests and fixtures for business assertions. Keep useful
verification code between tasks; a new script is not required for each check.
Discovery needs no credentials. Validate a changed binding with `loop check`, then
exercise the relevant operation against the authorised local fixture.

A named executable can be built later with `loop build`; use `loop build --help`
and select a pinned SDK or explicit development checkout. Publication is separate
from these local checks.
